1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,580
It's Friday, May the twelfth,

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:03,560
2017.

3
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:06,440
You walk into a hospital in England…

4
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:08,760
and every computer screen is red.

5
00:00:08,906 --> 00:00:11,286
A message says your files are locked.

6
00:00:11,586 --> 00:00:13,646
Pay three hundred dollars in bitcoin,

7
00:00:13,926 --> 00:00:15,306
or lose everything.

8
00:00:15,497 --> 00:00:17,216
And it's not just this hospital.

9
00:00:17,236 --> 00:00:20,167
It's happening in over a hundred countries.

10
00:00:20,287 --> 00:00:21,647
At the same time.

11
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:23,580
The person who stops it?

12
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,600
A twenty-two year old, in his bedroom,

13
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:26,760
on his week off.

14
00:00:26,993 --> 00:00:29,473
With ten dollars and sixty-nine cents.

15
00:00:30,073 --> 00:00:31,493
And three months later…

16
00:00:31,513 --> 00:00:33,273
the FBI arrests him.

17
00:00:33,562 --> 00:00:36,002
This is the story of the accidental hero

18
00:00:36,022 --> 00:00:38,882
who stopped a global cyberattack…

19
00:00:38,902 --> 00:00:41,042
and the secret that caught up with him.

20
00:00:43,014 --> 00:00:45,174
The attack was called WannaCry.

21
00:00:45,474 --> 00:00:46,494
And it was ransomware:

22
00:00:46,794 --> 00:00:49,154
software that locks up your files and

23
00:00:49,174 --> 00:00:50,534
holds them hostage.

24
00:00:50,923 --> 00:00:53,823
But WannaCry was worse than normal ransomware.

25
00:00:54,423 --> 00:00:56,783
It didn't wait for you to click a bad link.

26
00:00:56,923 --> 00:00:59,163
It spread by itself, from computer to

27
00:00:59,183 --> 00:01:02,263
computer, like a cold in a crowded room.

28
00:01:02,317 --> 00:01:04,897
It used a hacking tool reportedly built by

29
00:01:04,917 --> 00:01:07,677
America's National Security Agency,

30
00:01:07,757 --> 00:01:09,457
called EternalBlue.

31
00:01:09,677 --> 00:01:12,217
A month earlier, a group calling itself

32
00:01:12,237 --> 00:01:14,717
the Shadow Brokers had leaked it online.

33
00:01:14,944 --> 00:01:18,244
Microsoft had already released a fix in March.

34
00:01:18,564 --> 00:01:20,704
But huge numbers of computers had never

35
00:01:20,724 --> 00:01:21,984
installed it.

36
00:01:22,004 --> 00:01:24,044
Some were so old, they weren't even

37
00:01:24,064 --> 00:01:25,324
supported anymore.

38
00:01:25,582 --> 00:01:27,802
So WannaCry tore through them.

39
00:01:28,022 --> 00:01:30,082
More than two hundred thousand computers.

40
00:01:30,422 --> 00:01:31,302
Factories.

41
00:01:31,322 --> 00:01:32,402
Train stations.

42
00:01:32,582 --> 00:01:33,482
Phone companies.

43
00:01:33,876 --> 00:01:36,956
And in Britain, it hit the National Health Service.

44
00:01:37,296 --> 00:01:40,156
About a third of hospital trusts were disrupted.

45
00:01:40,556 --> 00:01:43,276
Around nineteen thousand appointments were cancelled.

46
00:01:43,906 --> 00:01:46,386
Meanwhile, in a small seaside town in

47
00:01:46,406 --> 00:01:47,226
England,

48
00:01:47,366 --> 00:01:49,786
a young man named Marcus Hutchins was on a

49
00:01:49,806 --> 00:01:50,726
week off.

50
00:01:50,853 --> 00:01:51,783
He was twenty-two.

51
00:01:51,803 --> 00:01:54,113
He'd taught himself most of what he knew

52
00:01:54,133 --> 00:01:55,313
about computers.

53
00:01:55,753 --> 00:01:58,113
And his job was taking malware apart,

54
00:01:58,133 --> 00:01:59,633
to see how it worked.

55
00:01:59,751 --> 00:02:02,371
He came back from lunch, saw the news,

56
00:02:02,391 --> 00:02:04,731
and got hold of a copy of WannaCry.

57
00:02:04,896 --> 00:02:07,416
And inside, he noticed something strange.

58
00:02:07,936 --> 00:02:09,716
Before attacking, the malware tried to

59
00:02:09,736 --> 00:02:11,696
visit a weird web address.

60
00:02:11,996 --> 00:02:14,476
A long, random jumble of letters.

61
00:02:14,838 --> 00:02:16,498
And nobody owned it.

62
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:19,220
Researchers sometimes buy addresses like

63
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:21,900
that, to watch what the malware does.

64
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:23,560
So Marcus bought it.

65
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:26,140
For ten dollars and sixty-nine cents.

66
00:02:26,856 --> 00:02:27,416
And then…

67
00:02:27,436 --> 00:02:29,476
the attack started to slow down.

68
00:02:30,146 --> 00:02:31,186
Here's why.

69
00:02:31,486 --> 00:02:32,806
Before infecting a computer,

70
00:02:32,826 --> 00:02:34,966
WannaCry checked that address.

71
00:02:35,066 --> 00:02:37,166
If nobody answered, it attacked.

72
00:02:37,286 --> 00:02:38,876
But if someone answered…

73
00:02:38,896 --> 00:02:40,516
…it switched itself off.

74
00:02:41,149 --> 00:02:42,889
Think of a burglar who knocks before

75
00:02:42,909 --> 00:02:44,189
breaking in.

76
00:02:44,309 --> 00:02:46,609
If nobody's home, he goes in.

77
00:02:46,729 --> 00:02:48,749
If someone answers, he runs.

78
00:02:49,569 --> 00:02:51,389
Marcus had just answered the door.

79
00:02:51,409 --> 00:02:53,349
For every computer in the world.

80
00:02:53,565 --> 00:02:55,345
He later said that, at first,

81
00:02:55,365 --> 00:02:56,625
it was an accident.

82
00:02:56,925 --> 00:02:58,525
He didn't know buying the address would

83
00:02:58,545 --> 00:02:59,566
stop it.

84
00:02:59,586 --> 00:03:01,254
Another researcher, Darien Huss,

85
00:03:01,274 --> 00:03:05,051
spotted the same switch at almost the same time.

86
00:03:05,071 --> 00:03:07,583
New versions of WannaCry appeared later.

87
00:03:07,603 --> 00:03:10,756
But the biggest wave had been stopped.

88
00:03:10,906 --> 00:03:13,046
Quick favour: if you like stories like

89
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:14,306
this, subscribe.

90
00:03:14,766 --> 00:03:17,086
It costs even less than ten dollars and

91
00:03:17,106 --> 00:03:18,506
sixty-nine cents.

92
00:03:18,928 --> 00:03:19,748
Overnight,

93
00:03:19,848 --> 00:03:22,348
Marcus became the accidental hero.

94
00:03:22,748 --> 00:03:24,848
Newspapers hunted for his real name,

95
00:03:24,868 --> 00:03:25,885
and found it.

96
00:03:25,905 --> 00:03:27,005
And the attackers?

97
00:03:27,425 --> 00:03:29,125
Very few victims paid.

98
00:03:29,365 --> 00:03:31,745
In the end, the ransom added up to only a

99
00:03:31,765 --> 00:03:34,225
little over a hundred thousand dollars.

100
00:03:34,421 --> 00:03:36,561
Months later, the American and British

101
00:03:36,581 --> 00:03:38,721
governments said they believed North Korea

102
00:03:38,741 --> 00:03:41,241
was behind WannaCry.

103
00:03:41,599 --> 00:03:43,879
For a while, Marcus was the most famous

104
00:03:43,899 --> 00:03:45,659
good hacker on the planet.

105
00:03:46,185 --> 00:03:48,465
Then, in August 2017,

106
00:03:48,785 --> 00:03:50,525
he flew to Las Vegas for one of the

107
00:03:50,545 --> 00:03:52,865
world's biggest hacker conferences,

108
00:03:53,145 --> 00:03:54,045
DEF CON.

109
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:56,400
At the airport, on his way home…

110
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:58,480
the FBI arrested him.

111
00:03:58,941 --> 00:04:00,421
Not for WannaCry.

112
00:04:00,741 --> 00:04:02,241
For something from years earlier,

113
00:04:02,261 --> 00:04:03,481
when he was a teenager.

114
00:04:03,908 --> 00:04:05,966
Prosecutors said that back then,

115
00:04:05,986 --> 00:04:08,874
he had helped create malware that stole

116
00:04:08,894 --> 00:04:09,705
online banking passwords.

117
00:04:09,725 --> 00:04:11,978
It was called Kronos.

118
00:04:12,253 --> 00:04:13,973
The hero who helped stop one of the

119
00:04:13,993 --> 00:04:16,553
biggest cyberattacks in history…

120
00:04:16,573 --> 00:04:19,333
could now face years in an American prison.

121
00:04:19,981 --> 00:04:24,081
In 2019, Marcus pleaded guilty to two charges.

122
00:04:24,501 --> 00:04:26,721
He said he regretted those mistakes,

123
00:04:26,861 --> 00:04:28,981
and that he'd spent years using his skills

124
00:04:29,001 --> 00:04:30,981
to protect people instead.

125
00:04:31,187 --> 00:04:33,067
And in July 2019,

126
00:04:33,347 --> 00:04:35,807
a judge made a decision that surprised a

127
00:04:35,827 --> 00:04:36,947
lot of people.

128
00:04:37,377 --> 00:04:38,437
No prison.

129
00:04:38,517 --> 00:04:40,477
Time already served, and one year of

130
00:04:40,497 --> 00:04:42,057
supervised release.

131
00:04:42,687 --> 00:04:45,007
The judge pointed to how young he was back

132
00:04:45,027 --> 00:04:48,027
then, how he had turned his life around…

133
00:04:48,047 --> 00:04:50,707
and the fact that he had helped stop WannaCry.

134
00:04:51,021 --> 00:04:52,661
Today, Marcus still works in

135
00:04:52,681 --> 00:04:53,801
cybersecurity,

136
00:04:54,121 --> 00:04:55,821
on the side of the defenders.

137
00:04:56,591 --> 00:04:58,131
So here's my question.

138
00:04:58,391 --> 00:05:00,771
Should one good deed wipe out a bad past?

139
00:05:01,011 --> 00:05:02,711
Or should the past still count?

140
00:05:02,951 --> 00:05:04,231
Tell me in the comments.

141
00:05:04,453 --> 00:05:06,653
And subscribe, because next time,

142
00:05:06,673 --> 00:05:09,313
there's another hacker story you won't believe.

143
00:05:09,618 --> 00:05:12,038
Ten dollars and sixty-nine cents.

144
00:05:12,278 --> 00:05:15,118
Probably the best money anyone ever spent.
